<div class="main-title">
  <div class="series">Likutay Halachos</div>
  <h1>Yoreh Dai'ah</h1>
  <div class="volume">The Laws of <em>Shechitah</em> — Section 4</div>
</div>

<div class="halacha-title">
  <h2>Shechitah 4</h2>
</div>

<!-- INTRODUCTORY CITATION -->
<div class="intro-citation">
Based on the Torah [discourse] <span class="verse">"Dirshu Hashem v'Uzo"</span> [Seek Hashem and His strength] in Siman 37, see there — which speaks there regarding the matter of <span class="key-term">shechitah</span>, see there, in Ois Vav what is said there. And this is [the meaning of]: <span class="verse">"Be careful regarding the veins, in accordance with Rabbi Yehudah"</span> — for the blood is the soul that comes in reincarnation [<span class="key-term">gilgul</span>], and the <span class="key-term">shochet</span> must have intention to elevate the soul that is in the blood. Also, the essential deficiency of <span class="key-term">parnasah</span> [livelihood], etc. <span class="ref">(see there the entire Torah [discourse] thoroughly)</span>.

<br><br>
And the general principle of the Torah [discourse] is that a person must subdue the <span class="key-term">chomer</span> [material/matter] toward the <span class="key-term">tzurah</span> [form] — to subdue the material and the body, which is the aspect of <span class="key-term">beheimah</span> [animal], the aspect of <span class="key-term">sichlus</span> [foolishness], the aspect of darkness, the aspect of death, the aspect of forgetfulness, the aspect of <span class="key-term">chochmos chitzoniyos</span> [external wisdoms]. And to strengthen and elevate the soul and the form, which is the aspect of life, the aspect of memory, the aspect of <span class="key-term">adam</span> [man], the aspect of <span class="key-term">chochmah</span> [wisdom], the aspect of light, the aspect of the wisdom of the Torah, etc. And this is merited through <span class="key-term">ta'anis</span> [fasting], which is the aspect of <span class="key-term">tzedakah</span> [charity]. And the essential thing is <span class="key-term">tzedakah</span> of Eretz Yisrael, which is the aspect of <em>the breath of the mouth that has no sin</em> — of the schoolchildren [<span class="key-term">tinokos shel bais rabban</span>] — for the essential sweetening of the <span class="key-term">din</span> [judgment], which is the aspect of darkness and forgetfulness and foolishness, etc., is through the breath of the mouth that has no sin, etc. And this is the aspect of <span class="key-term">shechitah</span>, which is the aspect of the elevation of the soul — to subdue the spirit of animality, the aspect of <span class="key-term">beheimah</span>, the aspect of the body, etc. — and to elevate the soul that is in the living creature to <span class="key-term">medaber</span> [the level of speech/human], which is the aspect of <span class="key-term">adam</span>, the aspect of Torah, etc. <span class="ref">(and see there all of this thoroughly)</span>. And see there at the end what is said there regarding the matter of the children of the <span class="key-term">amei ha'aretz</span> [ignorant people], etc. — and the entire Torah [discourse] that they reveal is entirely a revelation to the <span class="key-term">shochet</span>, etc. <span class="ref">(see there all of this thoroughly)</span>.
</div>

<!-- SECTION 1 -->
<div class="section-heading">Section 1</div>

<p>
And this is the aspect of the <strong>five laws of <span class="key-term">shechitah</span></strong> <span class="source">(Yoreh Dai'ah, Siman 23:1)</span>, corresponding to the five aspects that the soul possesses, which are the aspect of the <strong>five [types of] blood</strong>, the aspect of the <strong>five letters of <span class="key-term">Mantzepach</span></strong> [<span class="heb">מנצפ״ך</span> — the five final letters], as is brought there in the above-mentioned Torah [discourse]. For all of this needs to be rectified through the <span class="key-term">shechitah</span>. And therefore, there are five laws of <span class="key-term">shechitah</span> — which are <span class="key-term">derasah</span>, etc. — corresponding to the five aspects that the soul possesses, etc., as mentioned above. And when one is careful regarding the above-mentioned five things that invalidate the <span class="key-term">shechitah</span> — which are <span class="key-term">shehiyah</span>, <span class="key-term">derasah</span>, etc. — through this one merits to elevate the soul that is in the living creature to <span class="key-term">medaber</span>, which [soul] is comprised of five aspects, as mentioned above.
</p>

<!-- SECTION 2 -->
<div class="section-heading">Section 2</div>

<p>
And this is [the meaning of] what the <span class="key-term">shochet</span> must show his knife to a <span class="key-term">chacham</span>, as our Sages of blessed memory said. For the essential rectification of the <span class="key-term">shechitah</span> — which is to elevate the soul — is through the <span class="key-term">chacham</span> in Torah specifically. For the soul of holiness is the aspect of the wisdom of the Torah <span class="source">(as explained there in the above-mentioned Torah [discourse] many times)</span>. Therefore, it is impossible to accomplish the rectification of the <span class="key-term">shechitah</span> except through the <span class="key-term">chacham</span> in the wisdom of the Torah. For it is impossible to elevate the soul except through the wisdom of the Torah. And therefore, one must receive the knife of <span class="key-term">shechitah</span> from the <span class="key-term">chacham</span> specifically, in order that he receive power from him to accomplish the rectification of the <span class="key-term">shechitah</span> — which is to elevate the soul — which is impossible except through the <span class="key-term">chacham</span> in the wisdom of the Torah, as mentioned above.
</p>

<p>
Also, in this act of showing his knife to the <span class="key-term">chacham</span>, in this he accords honor to the <span class="key-term">chacham</span>. And as is explained in the words of our Sages of blessed memory, that one must show his knife to the <span class="key-term">chacham</span> because of the honor of the <span class="key-term">chacham</span>. And the essential elevation of the soul is through the <span class="key-term">kavod</span> [honor/glory] of holiness, which is the root of everything — the root of the Torah and the soul, etc. <span class="source">(as explained there)</span>. Therefore, one must show his knife to the <span class="key-term">chacham</span> because of the honor of the <span class="key-term">chacham</span>. And then the <span class="key-term">shochet</span> has a portion in the <span class="key-term">kavod</span> of holiness, in the aspect of: <span class="verse">"for those who honor Me, I shall honor"</span> <span class="source">(Shmuel I 2:30)</span>. And through this he can rectify and elevate the soul that is in the living creature, for the essential ascent of the soul is through the <span class="key-term">kavod</span> of holiness, which is the root of all the souls, as mentioned above.
</p>

<p>
And therefore, the slaughter of a non-Jew [renders the animal] a <span class="key-term">nevailah</span>, even if he does not worship idolatry <span class="source">(Yoreh Dai'ah, Siman 2:1)</span>. For the rectification of the <span class="key-term">shechitah</span> is to elevate the soul, which is the aspect of Torah. Therefore, it is impossible to accomplish this rectification except through a member of Yisrael who has a portion in the Torah — but not through a non-Jew. And likewise, a Jewish apostate [<span class="key-term">mumar</span>], etc. <span class="source">(ibid., 2:5)</span> — since he has already departed from the faith, his slaughter is a <span class="key-term">nevailah</span>. For the rectification of the <span class="key-term">shechitah</span> must be through one who has a portion in the Torah specifically — which is a valid Jew [<span class="key-term">Yisrael kasher</span>] — for then he has the power to elevate the soul, which is the aspect of Torah, as mentioned above.
</p>

<!-- SECTION 3 -->
<div class="section-heading">Section 3</div>

<p>
And this is [the meaning of]: <span class="verse">"it and its offspring you shall not slaughter on one day"</span> <span class="source">(Vayikra 22:28)</span>. For in truth, all the souls that are in domesticated animals and wild animals and birds are actual human souls that have been reincarnated in them <span class="source">(as is understood from the above-mentioned Torah [discourse] and from other places)</span>. And this is the essential rectification of the <span class="key-term">shechitah</span>: to elevate the soul that is in the living creature — that was reincarnated and descended from its level through its sins, from the aspect of <span class="key-term">adam</span> to <span class="key-term">beheimah</span> — to return to rectify it and restore it to its level, to elevate it from <span class="key-term">beheimah</span> to <span class="key-term">adam</span>.
</p>

<p>
Therefore, the <span class="key-term">shochet</span> must be a very righteous and G-d-fearing man. And he should be very careful with his soul to recite the blessing of <span class="key-term">shechitah</span> with great <span class="key-term">kavvanah</span> [intention], and to be very, very careful in all the laws of <span class="key-term">shechitah</span>, in order that he merit to rectify the soul and elevate it to its level — from <span class="key-term">beheimah</span> to <span class="key-term">adam</span>. And therefore, there are five laws of <span class="key-term">shechitah</span> corresponding to the five books of the Torah, which are the totality of the entire Torah. For the essential principle of the entire Torah is to elevate the soul, which is the aspect of <span class="key-term">adam</span>, etc. — which is the aspect of <span class="key-term">shechitah</span>, as mentioned above.
</p>

<p>
And this rectification — which is to elevate and clarify the souls from <span class="key-term">beheimah</span> to <span class="key-term">adam</span>, etc. — which is the aspect of <span class="key-term">shechitah</span>: in this rectification we are engaged all our days, every single day and in every single generation, until Mashiach comes — when the clarification will be completed, as is known <span class="source">(Aytz Chayyim, Sha'ar 39, Chapter 1; and Sha'ar 19, Chapter 3 and end of Chapter 5)</span>. For it is impossible to rectify and clarify everything on one day; rather, every single day and in every single generation, specific clarifications [<span class="key-term">beirurim</span>] are clarified, until the clarification is completed in the days of Mashiach <span class="source">(as is known from the writings of the Arizal there)</span>.
</p>

<p>
And the general principle is that no day is like the next, and Hashem, blessed is He, renews in His goodness every day, continuously, the work of creation. And each day, specific clarifications are made that it is impossible to rectify on another day. And similarly in every generation. And because of this, the exile is prolonged so long — until the clarification is completed <span class="source">(as all of this is explained in Sefer HaGilgulim and in the other writings of the Arizal)</span>.
</p>

<p>
And therefore there is an obligation on every person to bear children. For it is impossible to complete the clarification except from generation to generation — for each generation must rectify and clarify specific clarifications according to the day and according to the generation. For every single day is the aspect of a specific generation, as our Sages of blessed memory said in the Midrash <span class="source">(Koheles Rabbah 1:5)</span>, on the verse: <span class="verse">"a generation goes and a generation comes"</span> <span class="source">(Koheles 1:4)</span> — there is no day in which sixty <span class="key-term">ribo</span> [ten-thousands, i.e. six hundred thousand] do not die and sixty <span class="key-term">ribo</span> are not born. It follows that every single day a new generation is renewed, which is six hundred thousand souls — for a generation is no fewer than six hundred thousand. And this is [the meaning of] what is juxtaposed in Koheles: <span class="verse">"the sun rises and the sun sets; a generation goes and a generation comes"</span> <span class="source">(Koheles 1:4–5)</span> — for just as the sun rises and the sun sets every single day, so too a new generation is renewed every single day, which is the aspect of <span class="verse">"a generation goes and a generation comes"</span> every day, as mentioned above.
</p>

<p>
And this is what is stated in <span class="key-term">Krias Sh'ma</span>: <span class="verse">"and these words that I command you today shall be upon your heart, and you shall teach them to your children"</span> <span class="source">(Devarim 6:6–7)</span> — that <span class="verse">"and you shall teach them to your children"</span> is juxtaposed to <span class="verse">"that I command you today"</span>. For the two are one. For <span class="verse">"that I command you today"</span> — <span class="verse">"today"</span> specifically — this is a warning that one must exert oneself every single day with renewed effort to fulfill the Torah and the <span class="key-term">mitzvos</span>. For every single day a person's <span class="key-term">yetzer</span> [evil inclination] overpowers him anew — literally — because every single day one must make new clarifications through Torah and divine service. And corresponding to every clarification, there are many <span class="key-term">klipos</span> [husks] and <span class="key-term">sitrin achranin</span> [other sides, forces of impurity] that surround them. For the holy sparks have fallen into the deepest depths of the <span class="key-term">klipos</span>. And because every day one must clarify new clarifications from the depths of the <span class="key-term">klipos</span>, therefore the Adversary [<span class="key-term">ba'al davar</span>] overpowers and wages war against the person anew every day. And therefore the Torah warns: <span class="verse">"that I command you today"</span> — <span class="verse">"today"</span> specifically — as our Sages of blessed memory expounded <span class="source">(Sifrei there, 33; brought in the commentary of Rashi; and see there on verse 7; Tanchuma 2, Yisro 13:10; and Rashi on Ki Savo 26:15)</span>: every single day they should be in your eyes as new. For certainly the words of Torah are literally new every single day. For the words of the Torah — which are the aspect of the elevation of the souls, as mentioned above — are renewed every single day, for every single day one must make new clarifications, as mentioned above.
</p>

<p>
And therefore, juxtaposed to this is: <span class="verse">"and you shall teach them to your children"</span> — that is, one must teach the Torah to his children, in order to reveal His G-dliness, blessed is He, and the holiness of His Torah from generation to generation, in order to complete the clarification of the sparks and the souls in every generation that pertains to them, until Mashiach comes, as mentioned above. And therefore it is juxtaposed to <span class="verse">"that I command you today"</span> — for the two are literally one. For the clarification of the sparks and the souls is needed every day specifically — anew — and likewise in every generation — anew. For every day is the aspect of a new generation, in the aspect of <span class="verse">"the sun rises and the sun sets; a generation goes and a generation comes,"</span> etc., as mentioned above.
</p>

<p>
For the essential clarification of the sparks and souls is for the sake of His honor, blessed is He. For the <span class="key-term">kavod</span> is the root of everything, for everything was created for the sake of His honor <span class="source">(Yeshayahu 43:7)</span> — <em>in order that they should know Him</em>, as mentioned above. And the knowledge of the honor of His G-dliness and His greatness, blessed is He, is revealed every single day in a new revelation, in the aspect of: <span class="verse">"the heavens declare the glory of G-d,"</span> etc.; <span class="verse">"day after day utters speech,"</span> etc. <span class="source">(Tehillim 19:2–3)</span>. And this is the aspect of: <span class="verse">"days shall speak"</span> <span class="source">(Iyov 32:7)</span> — for every single day utters and speaks the honor of His G-dliness, blessed is He, according to the clarification of the sparks on that day. And this is the aspect of the renewal of the work of creation that occurs every day, as mentioned above.
</p>

<!-- SECTION 4 -->
<div class="section-heading">Section 4</div>

<p>
And this is the aspect of the <strong>prohibition of <span class="key-term">oso v'es beno</span></strong> — not to slaughter [it and its offspring] on one day. For just as among human beings one must clarify from day to day specifically, and from generation to generation specifically, so too among domesticated animals and wild animals, etc. — one needs new clarifications every day and in every generation. For it is all one, for also the souls that are in the living creature are human souls that were reincarnated in them, as mentioned above. And therefore, Hashem, blessed is He, also decreed upon them: <span class="verse">"be fruitful and multiply,"</span> etc. — in order to complete the clarification every day and in every generation, as mentioned above.
</p>

<p>
And the essential clarification of the souls of animals is through the <span class="key-term">shechitah</span>, as mentioned above. For specifically then, at the time of the <span class="key-term">shechitah</span>, the soul that is in the living creature departs and ascends to its level — from <span class="key-term">chai</span> [living creature] to <span class="key-term">medaber</span> [speaking/human], as mentioned above. Therefore, the Torah commanded not to slaughter it and its offspring on one day. For the clarification of the animal itself through the <span class="key-term">shechitah</span> and the clarification of its offspring are two specific clarifications, which are the aspect of specific days, as mentioned above — for the change of days and the change of generations are one and the same aspect. And therefore, it is certainly forbidden to slaughter them both on one day, for it is impossible to clarify the clarification of two generations on one day.
</p>

<p>
And if, G-d forbid, one wishes to clarify the clarification of two specific generations on one day, the <span class="key-term">Sitra Achra</span> [the Other Side] will overpower even more than at the beginning — since he wants to clarify out of its proper time. Therefore, since the essential rectification and clarification of the living creature is through the <span class="key-term">shechitah</span>, therefore it is forbidden to slaughter it and its offspring on one day. For it is impossible to clarify the clarification of two specific generations on one day, for every single day is the aspect of a specific generation, as mentioned above.
</p>

<!-- SECTION 5 -->
<div class="section-heading">Section 5</div>

<p>
And therefore, the essential complete <span class="key-term">ta'anis</span> [fast] is no less than one day — like the fast of Yom HaKipurim and the fasts that our Sages of blessed memory decreed, all of which have their measure as one day. And likewise, all the fasts that people accept upon themselves for <span class="key-term">teshuvah</span> — all of their measure is no less than one day. For the essential rectification and elevation of the soul is through <span class="key-term">ta'anis</span> <span class="source">(as explained in the above-mentioned Torah [discourse])</span>. And therefore, the measure of every fast is at least one day. For the essential rectification is in the aspect of <em>days</em>, for there is the essential place of blemish and rectification. For the days are the aspect of <span class="key-term">middos</span> [character traits/attributes], in the aspect of: <span class="verse">"the measure of my days, what is it?"</span> <span class="source">(Tehillim 39:5)</span> — as Rabbeinu of blessed memory wrote elsewhere.
</p>

<p>
And there, in the aspect of the <span class="key-term">middos</span> — which are in the aspect of the days — there is the essential place of all blemishes and rectifications, as is known. And therefore, each and every person, according to his blemishes through his sins, so must he fast for <span class="key-term">teshuvah</span> <span class="source">(as brought in the holy books; see Sha'ar Ruach HaKoidesh of the Arizal, Tikkunim 16–22)</span> — in order to rectify and clarify the blemishes of his soul, each day in its day. And therefore, the measure of a fast is no less than a day, for the rectification and clarification is in the aspect of a day, as mentioned above.
</p>

<!-- SECTION 6 -->
<div class="section-heading">Section 6</div>

<p>
And this is the aspect of the <strong><span class="key-term">shekalim</span></strong> [half-shekels] that were commanded in order to atone for the sin of the <span class="key-term">eigel</span> [golden calf], which includes all sins — for then the <span class="key-term">zuhama</span> [spiritual contamination] returned, etc. And they were commanded after Yom HaKipurim to give <span class="key-term">shekalim</span> for atonement, as it is stated: <span class="verse">"to atone for your souls"</span> <span class="source">(Shemos 30:16)</span>. This is the aspect of <span class="key-term">"agra d'sa'anisa tzidkasa"</span> [the reward of the fast is the charity] <span class="source">(Berachos 6b)</span> — that the essential [purpose of the] fast is the <span class="key-term">tzedakah</span> of Eretz Yisrael, through which one elevates the soul and subdues the body, etc. <span class="source">(as explained in the above-mentioned Torah [discourse])</span>.
</p>

<p>
And therefore, after Yom HaKipurim, which is the holy fast day, afterward they were commanded regarding the <span class="key-term">shekalim</span> <span class="source">(in the commentary of Rashi, Shemos 30:16)</span> — which are a free-will donation of the heart, the aspect of <span class="key-term">tzedakah</span>, the aspect of <span class="key-term">"agra d'sa'anisa tzidkasa."</span> And all of it is in order to rectify the sin of the <span class="key-term">eigel</span>, which includes all sins — in order to subdue the material and the body, and to rectify and elevate the soul, in the aspect of <span class="verse">"to atone for your souls"</span> — <span class="verse">"for your souls"</span> specifically.
</p>

<p>
For the <span class="key-term">shekalim</span> are the aspect of <span class="key-term">tzedakah</span> of Eretz Yisrael, which is the aspect of the breath of the mouth that has no sin — of the schoolchildren [<span class="key-term">tinokos shel bais rabban</span>] <span class="source">(Shabbos 119b)</span>. For the essential elevation of the soul is through this, as mentioned above. For the <span class="key-term">shekalim</span> were for the purpose of the donation for the <span class="key-term">Mishkan</span> [Tabernacle]. And it is known that the <span class="key-term">Mishkan</span> was in order to draw down and reveal the holiness of Eretz Yisrael — whose essential holiness derives from the Bais HaMikdash [Holy Temple], which is the aspect of the <span class="key-term">Mishkan</span>. For the <span class="key-term">Mishkan</span> and the Bais HaMikdash are one and the same aspect, as our Sages of blessed memory said: <em>"We find that the Mikdash is called Mishkan."</em>
</p>

<p>
And in every place where the <span class="key-term">Mishkan</span> encamped, there were all the laws of the holiness of the Bais HaMikdash regarding the distancing of non-<span class="key-term">kohanim</span> and impure people, etc. <span class="source">(see Bamidbar 5:2)</span>. For in the <span class="key-term">Mishkan</span> there was the holiness of the Bais HaMikdash, which is the aspect of the holiness of Eretz Yisrael, which is the aspect of the holy breath of the schoolchildren — who receive their holy breath from the <span class="key-term">Mishkan</span>, from the Holy of Holies, where the <span class="key-term">keruvim</span> [cherubim] stood — which are <span class="key-term">anpay zutray</span> [small faces], the aspect of children <span class="source">(as brought in the above-mentioned Torah [discourse], and as explained in the Torah [discourse] "Azamrah l'Elokai b'Odi" in Siman 282)</span> — for the children receive their breath from the <span class="key-term">Mishkan</span> of each and every <span class="key-term">tzadik</span>, etc.
</p>

<p>
Therefore, through the <span class="key-term">tzedakah</span> of the <span class="key-term">shekalim</span> for the <span class="key-term">Mishkan</span> — which is the aspect of the holiness of Eretz Yisrael, the aspect of the breath of the schoolchildren — through this one merits to subdue the material and the body and to elevate the soul, as mentioned above. And this is the rectification of all sins, as mentioned above, for all sins are drawn from the <span class="key-term">zuhama</span> of the body that returned through the sin of the <span class="key-term">eigel</span>, as is known.
</p>

<!-- SECTION 7 -->
<div class="section-heading">Section 7</div>

<p>
And therefore, the essential subduing of Haman-Amalek was through the <span class="key-term">shekalim</span>, as our Sages of blessed memory said: <em>"Wicked one! Their shekalim already preceded your shekalim."</em> For Amalek is the <span class="key-term">Sitra Achra</span> <span class="source">(Zohar, Ki Sayzay 281a)</span>, which strengthens itself greatly every single day — anew. And likewise in every single generation — anew. According to the clarifications that must be made every day and in every generation anew, so too, corresponding to this, Amalek strengthens itself every day and in every generation anew. And therefore: <span class="verse">"Hashem wages war against Amalek from generation to generation"</span> <span class="source">(Shemos 17:16)</span> — <span class="verse">"from generation to generation"</span> specifically — for in every single generation, Hashem, blessed is He, must fight against him anew, as mentioned above.
</p>

<p>
And therefore, the essential subduing of Haman-Amalek is through <span class="key-term">ta'anis</span>. And therefore Esther decreed a fast, as it is stated: <span class="verse">"and fast for me"</span> <span class="source">(Esther 4:16)</span>. And she inscribed the remembrance of the fast for [all] generations, as it is stated: <span class="verse">"and the matters of the fasts and their outcry"</span> <span class="source">(Esther 9:31)</span> — to know and to make known that the essential subduing of Haman-Amalek — who is the <span class="key-term">Sitra Achra</span> in every generation — is through <span class="key-term">ta'anis</span>. And likewise, in the days of Moshe, his [Amalek's] subduing was through fasting, as our Sages of blessed memory explained: <span class="verse">"and Moshe, Aharon, and Chur went up,"</span> etc. <span class="source">(Shemos 17:10)</span> — they were immersed in a fast.
</p>

<p>
And therefore, the essential subduing of Haman-Amalek was through the <span class="key-term">shekalim</span>, for the essential [purpose of the] fast is the <span class="key-term">tzedakah</span>, the aspect of <span class="key-term">"agra d'sa'anisa tzidkasa,"</span> as mentioned above — which is the aspect of <span class="key-term">shekalim</span>, as mentioned above.
</p>

<!-- SECTION 8 -->
<div class="section-heading">Section 8</div>

<p>
And therefore, Haman the wicked — may his name be blotted out — cast a <span class="key-term">pur</span> [lot] from day to day and from month to month, etc. <span class="source">(Esther 3:7)</span>, until the lot fell for him on the thirteenth of Adar. For Haman was wise in evil and a great sorcerer, and he knew that the clarifications are made every single day according to the day and according to the generation. And therefore, in his wicked wisdom, he cast lots in order to determine the month and the day on which he could overpower Yisrael, G-d forbid. And certainly his lot was no empty matter, and certainly he aimed well at the specific day when it was seemingly impossible to withstand him <span class="source">(see Esther Rabbah 7:13)</span>.
</p>

<p>
But Hashem, blessed is He, is mighty over all, and He performed for us great and awesome miracles and wonders, and He helped and saved Mordechai and Esther through the power of the fasts and their outcry, to subdue Haman the wicked and all the seed of Amalek on that very day. And it was reversed from one extreme to the other, as it is stated: <span class="verse">"and it was reversed"</span> <span class="source">(Esther 9:1)</span>. And in truth, it was a wondrous miracle without compare — that the <span class="key-term">pur</span> and the lot were reversed from one extreme to the other, literally — for specifically on that day, the seed of Amalek was killed and destroyed. And therefore, these days were called <span class="verse">"Purim"</span> — on account of the <span class="key-term">pur</span> <span class="source">(Esther 9:26)</span> — for this is the essential force of this wondrous and awesome miracle: that the <span class="key-term">pur</span> and the lot of Haman — who was an extraordinary sorcerer and so wise in evil, and aimed very, very carefully with his lot at that specific day, which was truly a time of great distress, and it was impossible to stand against him on that day, so that even the heavenly hosts concurred with the decree, G-d forbid — but a boorish man does not know that <span class="verse">"very deep are Your thoughts"</span> <span class="source">(Tehillim 92:6–7)</span>, blessed is He, and there is no despair in the world at all!
</p>

<p>
For even though in truth that day was suited for his evil purpose, G-d forbid, and it was very, very difficult to stand against him — until almost, almost, all the enemies of Yisrael would have been destroyed, G-d forbid — nevertheless, <span class="verse">"You, Hashem, are exalted forever"</span> <span class="source">(Tehillim 92:9)</span>. And even if a sharp sword is placed upon a person's neck, he should not withhold himself from [seeking] mercy. For through the fasting and the <span class="key-term">ta'anis</span> that Mordechai and Esther decreed, the matter was reversed from one extreme to the other. And the lot and the <span class="key-term">pur</span> were reversed — to fell him and his seed and his family on that very day. For this is known <span class="source">(see Yerushalmi, Ta'anis, Chapter 1, Halachah 1; and see Likutay Moharan 33:2 and Siman 115; and Part II, Siman 12)</span>: that in every place where the <span class="key-term">Sitra Achra</span> has the power to strengthen itself more and more, there is certainly hidden and concealed there a very, very lofty holiness — only it is in great, great hiddenness and concealment, until it seems that it is impossible in any way to subdue that <span class="key-term">Sitra Achra</span> and <span class="key-term">klipah</span>. But in truth, if you desire, you can overpower it — only greater exertion is needed, in fasting and <span class="key-term">ta'anis</span> and crying out and pleading and prayer and many supplications, as in the days of Mordechai and Esther.
</p>

<p>
And then one can merit that the matter be reversed from one extreme to the other — as it was then — that specifically on that day they felled him, and the <span class="key-term">pur</span> and the lot were reversed from one extreme to the other. Therefore they called these days <span class="verse">"Purim"</span> — specifically. And so it is in every generation and in every day, as it is stated: <span class="verse">"and these days are remembered and observed in every generation"</span> <span class="source">(Esther 9:28)</span>, etc.
</p>

<p>
<strong>And understand these words very well for practical application, for your eternal life depends on this</strong>: that you should believe and know, every day and at every time and in every place, that you still have the power — however it may be — to overcome whatever you need to overcome on that very day and place. And Hashem, blessed is He, will help and save you. Amen.
</p>

<!-- SECTION 9 -->
<div class="section-heading">Section 9</div>

<p>
And this is the aspect of the <strong>eighteen <span class="key-term">treifos</span></strong> [non-kosher conditions] — that it is forbidden to eat the <span class="key-term">treifah</span> that is not viable. For Hashem, blessed is He, created man with a body and a soul, matter and form — which are the aspects of <span class="key-term">adam</span> and <span class="key-term">beheimah</span>, etc. — in order that he should have free choice, in order that he should exert himself to subdue and break the body and the material, the aspect of <span class="key-term">beheimah</span>, and to elevate the soul, the aspect of <span class="key-term">adam</span>, etc. For this was the essential purpose of the creation of man, as is known. And once man was created, everything was given into the hand of man — for he specifically must clarify everything.
</p>

<p>
Therefore, it is impossible to subdue the spirit of animality of the animal — <span class="verse">"the spirit of the animal that descends downward to the earth"</span> <span class="source">(Koheles 3:21)</span> — except through man, who specifically has the power to clarify the spirit of the animal and elevate it from <span class="key-term">beheimah</span> to <span class="key-term">adam</span>. Therefore, if the animal dies by itself — by the Hand of Heaven — it is a <span class="key-term">nevailah</span>. And even if it is a <span class="key-term">treifah</span> that is not viable, it is considered as dead and forbidden to eat. For Hashem, blessed is He, gave everything over into our hands — we who possess free choice — for we, the sons of man, specifically, must extract the soul from the animal through the <span class="key-term">shechitah</span>.
</p>

<p>
And then, specifically — when the <span class="key-term">shochet</span> fulfills the mitzvah of <span class="key-term">shechitah</span> properly — then specifically, the soul is clarified from the animal and elevated from <span class="key-term">beheimah</span> to <span class="key-term">adam</span>, from <span class="key-term">chai</span> to <span class="key-term">medaber</span>. For only man has this power, for man was created for this purpose: that he should have this battle — to subdue the body, which is the aspect of <span class="key-term">beheimah</span>, as mentioned above.
</p>

<p>
And this is the aspect of the knife of <span class="key-term">shechitah</span> — this is the aspect of <span class="key-term">ta'anis</span>. For the essential subduing of the body, the aspect of <span class="key-term">beheimah</span>, is through <span class="key-term">ta'anis</span>, as mentioned above — through which one ascends from <span class="key-term">beheimah</span> to <span class="key-term">adam</span>. And the essential boundary of man is <span class="key-term">dibbur</span> [speech], for man has speech <span class="source">(see Targum on Bereishis 2:7)</span>, and an animal has no speech — in the aspect of: <span class="verse">"he is likened to the silenced animals"</span> <span class="source">(Tehillim 49:13)</span>. And through the <span class="key-term">ta'anis</span> one merits to emerge from <span class="key-term">beheimah</span> to <span class="key-term">adam</span>, as mentioned above — that is, one merits the aspect of <span class="key-term">dibbur</span>, which is the boundary of man.
</p>

<p>
And this is the aspect of the knife of <span class="key-term">shechitah</span>, in the aspect of: <span class="verse">"the exaltations of G-d in their throat and a double-edged sword in their hand"</span> <span class="source">(Tehillim 149:6)</span>. For the <span class="key-term">Shechinah</span>, which is the aspect of <span class="key-term">dibbur</span>, the aspect of <span class="verse">"Hashem, open my lips"</span> <span class="source">(Tehillim 51:17)</span> — is the aspect of <span class="verse">"a sword for Hashem"</span> <span class="source">(as mentioned in the above-mentioned Torah [discourse])</span> — which is the aspect of the knife of <span class="key-term">shechitah</span> <span class="source">(as explained there)</span>. It follows that the knife of <span class="key-term">shechitah</span>, which is the aspect of <span class="verse">"a sword for Hashem,"</span> is the aspect of <span class="key-term">dibbur</span> — which is the aspect of <span class="key-term">ta'anis</span>, through which one merits <span class="key-term">dibbur</span>, to emerge from <span class="key-term">beheimah</span> to <span class="key-term">adam</span> — which is <span class="key-term">dibbur</span>, as mentioned above.
</p>

<p>
For in the body and the material, the aspect of <span class="key-term">beheimah</span> — there is the hold of the <span class="key-term">yetzer hara</span> [evil inclination] and the <span class="key-term">Samech Mem</span> [Satan], which is the aspect of Amalek, the aspect of Eisav — who is the <span class="key-term">zuhama</span> of the <span class="key-term">nachash</span> [serpent]. And he is appointed over the sword, in the aspect of: <span class="verse">"and by your sword shall you live"</span> <span class="source">(Bereishis 27:40)</span>. And his essential subduing is through <span class="key-term">ta'anis</span>, which is harder than the sword, as is brought in <em>Sefer HaAlef-Beis</em>, in the section on <span class="key-term">Teshuvah</span>, letter Gimmel: <em>"the fast is harder than the sword."</em> For the essential [purpose of the] fast is the <span class="key-term">tzedakah</span> — that is, <span class="key-term">tzedakah</span> of Eretz Yisrael — which is the aspect of breath without sin, the aspect of the breath of the schoolchildren. For every person must merit this: to exert himself to break the material and the body through the <span class="key-term">ta'anis</span> until he merits that he have breath of the mouth that has no sin — like the schoolchildren.
</p>

<!-- SECTION 10 -->
<div class="section-heading">Section 10</div>

<p>
For the essential subduing of Eisav, the aspect of Amalek, is through the voice of the schoolchildren, as our Sages of blessed memory expounded on the verse: <span class="verse">"the voice is the voice of Yaakov and the hands are the hands of Eisav"</span> <span class="source">(Bereishis 27:22)</span> — when the voice is the voice of Yaakov, when the schoolchildren chirp with their voice, the hands are not the hands of Eisav. And from there — from this aspect — the <span class="key-term">shochet</span> must receive the sword of <span class="key-term">shechitah</span>, as is explained in the above-mentioned Torah [discourse]: that the <span class="key-term">shochet</span> must have breath of the mouth that has no sin — like the schoolchildren. For through the <span class="key-term">ta'anis</span> — through which one merits the breath of the schoolchildren that has no sin — from there one receives the power to overpower Amalek with the sword specifically, in the aspect of: <span class="verse">"and Yehoshua weakened Amalek and his people <em>by the edge of the sword</em>"</span> <span class="source">(Shemos 17:13)</span> — <span class="verse">"by the edge of the sword"</span> specifically. For <span class="key-term">"minay u'vay abba laizil bay narga"</span> [from itself and within itself, the axe goes against it] — for all his power is the sword, as mentioned above. Therefore, through the <span class="key-term">ta'anis</span>, through which one subdues him, through this one takes from his hand the sword specifically and subdues him with the sword specifically.
</p>

<p>
For then, in the war against Amalek, they were immersed in a fast, as our Sages of blessed memory expounded on the verse: <span class="verse">"and Moshe, Aharon, and Chur,"</span> etc. — they were immersed in a fast, as mentioned above. And likewise, in the days of Mordechai they decreed a fast, as it is stated: <span class="verse">"and the matters of the fasts,"</span> etc. And as mentioned above — for the fast is harder than the sword, as mentioned above. For through the <span class="key-term">ta'anis</span> one overpowers the body, where Amalek has his hold. And one takes from him the sword and subdues him with the sword specifically — as it was in the days of Mordechai, when the matter was reversed and they killed Haman and the seed of Amalek with the sword. And it was all through the power of the <span class="key-term">ta'anis</span>, the aspect of <span class="verse">"and fast for me,"</span> as mentioned above.
</p>

<p>
And this is the aspect of the knife of <span class="key-term">shechitah</span>, which is drawn in its root from this aspect — from the aspect of subduing the material and the body, the aspect of <span class="key-term">beheimah</span>, through the <span class="key-term">ta'anis</span>. From there one receives the power of the knife of <span class="key-term">shechitah</span> — to subdue through it the aspect of <span class="key-term">beheimah</span> and to elevate the soul from <span class="key-term">chai</span> to <span class="key-term">medaber</span>, as mentioned above.
</p>

<!-- SECTION 11 -->
<div class="section-heading">Section 11</div>

<p>
And through this you will see wonders: that which we find that our Sages of blessed memory <span class="source">(Chullin 12a)</span> derived the law of <strong>inspecting the knife</strong> from the verse: <span class="verse">"and you shall slaughter with this"</span> <span class="source">(Shmuel I 14:34)</span>. For this verse was stated regarding Shaul after they had fasted on that day <span class="source">(as explained there in the context of Shmuel I, Chapter 14)</span>. For the essential rectification of the <span class="key-term">shechitah</span> is drawn from the aspect of <span class="key-term">ta'anis</span>, as mentioned above.
</p>

<p><em>[Note: This pertains to what was said above.]</em></p>

<!-- SECTION 12 -->
<div class="section-heading">Section 12</div>

<p>
Regarding the <strong>prohibition of <span class="key-term">nevailah</span> and <span class="key-term">treifah</span></strong> — that is not viable and is therefore considered as dead: for the rectification of the <span class="key-term">shechitah</span> is to subdue the aspect of the spirit of animality, which is the aspect of the side of the body, the aspect of death, etc. And therefore, an animal that dies by itself is forbidden, for the Side of Death [<span class="key-term">Sitra d'Mosa</span>] has dominated it, and then the spirit of animality overpowers. For it is impossible for Yisrael to eat from the animal except through the <span class="key-term">shechitah</span>, which is the aspect of <span class="key-term">ta'anis</span>, through which one subdues the spirit of animality, the aspect of death, the aspect of body, and elevates the soul from <span class="key-term">chai</span> to <span class="key-term">medaber</span>, from <span class="key-term">beheimah</span> to <span class="key-term">adam</span>, etc., as mentioned above.
</p>

<p>
For to subdue and nullify the Side of Death from the animal — this is impossible except through man, the possessor of free choice, specifically, as mentioned above. For man has free choice, and therefore he has the power to nullify the aspect of the Side of Death from himself — through sanctifying his body and subduing the body and the material, which are the aspect of death, toward the soul, which is the aspect of life, etc., as mentioned above. And therefore the Torah and the <span class="key-term">mitzvos</span> are called <span class="verse">"life,"</span> as it is stated: <span class="verse">"and he shall live by them"</span> <span class="source">(Vayikra 18:5)</span>; <span class="verse">"for it is your life,"</span> etc. <span class="source">(Devarim 30:20)</span>. And therefore, <span class="key-term">tzadikim</span> — even in their death they are called living — for they nullified the Side of Death from themselves.
</p>

<p>
But an animal has no free choice, and its essential rectification — to nullify the Side of Death from it so that it be fit for the consumption of Yisrael — is through man, the possessor of free choice, specifically, as mentioned above.
</p>

<!-- SECTION 13 -->
<div class="section-heading">Section 13</div>

<p>
And this is the aspect of the <strong>four <span class="key-term">parshiyos</span></strong> [Torah portions] that are read in Adar <span class="source">(Orach Chayyim, Siman 685)</span>, close to Purim and Pesach. For Purim and Pesach are the totality of all the redemptions. For Pesach is a remembrance of the Exodus from Mitzrayim, which includes all the exiles — for all the exiles are referred to by the name Mitzrayim <span class="source">(as brought elsewhere)</span>. And the miracle of Purim is even greater still, for it is the downfall of Haman-Amalek, who is <span class="verse">"the first of the nations,"</span> etc., as is known.
</p>

<p>
And the essential subduing of Mitzrayim and Amalek — who are the totality of the <span class="key-term">Sitra Achra</span> — is to subdue and nullify the <span class="key-term">zuhama</span> of the <span class="key-term">nachash</span> [serpent] that clings to the body, the aspect of <span class="key-term">beheimah</span> — from which the hold of the <span class="key-term">Sitra Achra</span> and the <span class="key-term">klipos</span>, which are the aspect of Amalek and Mitzrayim. And therefore, there are four kingdoms [<span class="key-term">malchuyos</span>] that are included in Amalek — who is <span class="verse">"the first of the nations"</span> — as Rabbeinu of blessed memory explained elsewhere <span class="source">(in Siman 30)</span>. For their hold is from the evil of the body — namely, from the evil of the four elements.
</p>

<p>
And therefore, before Pesach and Purim, the four <span class="key-term">parshiyos</span> are read — in order to subdue and nullify the four kingdoms, to subdue and nullify the evil in the four elements of the body through the holiness of the <span class="key-term">ta'anis</span>. And therefore, the essential beginning of the four <span class="key-term">parshiyos</span> is <strong>Parshas Shekalim</strong> — that they gave <span class="key-term">shekalim</span> as a donation for the <span class="key-term">Mishkan</span>, which is <span class="key-term">tzedakah</span> and donation — which is the aspect of <span class="key-term">ta'anis</span>, the aspect of <span class="key-term">"agra d'sa'anisa tzidkasa"</span> <span class="source">(Berachos 6b)</span>.
</p>

<p>
And therefore, in the <span class="key-term">Mishkan</span>, all the <span class="key-term">korbanos</span> were brought — which are the aspect of subduing the spirit of animality — through which all the sins are rectified, which are drawn from the aspect of animality, body, darkness, death, etc., as mentioned above. For everything is subdued and nullified through the aspect of the <span class="key-term">Mishkan</span> — which came from the donations of Yisrael, the aspect of <span class="key-term">tzedakah</span> that they donated after the <span class="key-term">ta'anis</span> of Yom HaKipurim, as mentioned above — the aspect of <span class="key-term">"agra d'sa'anisa tzidkasa."</span> For through this is the essential subduing of the body, the aspect of <span class="key-term">beheimah</span>, the aspect of death, etc., as mentioned above. And this is what is stated regarding the <span class="key-term">shekalim</span>: <span class="verse">"and there shall not be a plague among them"</span> <span class="source">(Shemos 30:12)</span> — for the Side of Death is nullified, which is the aspect of body, etc., as mentioned above.
</p>

<p>
And afterward, <strong>Parshas Zachor</strong> is read <span class="source">(end of Parshas Ki Sayzay)</span> — the obliteration of Amalek. For Amalek is the <span class="key-term">zuhama</span> of the <span class="key-term">nachash</span>, the Side of Death, the aspect of body, the aspect of <span class="key-term">beheimah</span>, as mentioned above. And therefore, they would disguise themselves as animals, as our Sages of blessed memory expounded. And one must subdue him through the <span class="key-term">shekalim</span>, which are the aspect of <span class="key-term">tzedakah</span>, the aspect of <span class="key-term">ta'anis</span>, as our Sages of blessed memory said: <em>"Their shekalim already preceded your shekalim,"</em> as mentioned above. And therefore they decreed a fast in the days of Mordechai and Esther, and likewise in the days of Moshe they were immersed in a fast in the war against Amalek — for his essential subduing is through the <span class="key-term">ta'anis</span>, as mentioned above. And this is: <span class="verse">"Remember what Amalek did to you"</span> <span class="source">(Devarim 25:17)</span> — <span class="verse">"Remember"</span> specifically — to strengthen <em>memory</em> over <em>forgetfulness</em>. For memory and forgetfulness are the aspects of soul and body, life and death, <span class="key-term">adam</span> and <span class="key-term">beheimah</span>, light and darkness, etc. — as mentioned above in the above-mentioned Torah [discourse].
</p>

<p>
Afterward, <strong>Parshas Parah</strong> is read <span class="source">(Bamidbar 19:1–22)</span> — which purifies from the impurity of the dead, to subdue the Side of Death, which is the aspect of body, the aspect of <span class="key-term">beheimah</span>, etc., as mentioned above. And afterward, <strong>Parshas HaChodesh</strong> is read <span class="source">(Shemos 12:1–20)</span> — which is the mitzvah of <span class="key-term">kiddush hachodesh</span> [sanctification of the new moon], to intercalate years and establish months, to harmonize the solar years with the lunar [months] — which is the aspect of filling the blemish of the moon. And then the darkness will be nullified, for the essential darkness of the night is due to the blemish of the moon. But in the future, when the blemish of the moon is filled, the darkness will be nullified, as it is stated: <span class="verse">"your sun shall no longer set, nor shall your moon be gathered in,"</span> etc. <span class="source">(Yeshayahu 60:20)</span>. It follows that through Parshas HaChodesh, the aspect of the mitzvah of <span class="key-term">kiddush hachodesh</span>, one subdues and nullifies the darkness, which is the aspect of body, the aspect of <span class="key-term">beheimah</span>, etc.
</p>

<p>
It follows that all four <span class="key-term">parshiyos</span> are to subdue the evil in the four elements — which are the aspect of death and darkness, <span class="key-term">beheimah</span>, body, forgetfulness, etc. — and to strengthen the soul, the aspect of <span class="key-term">adam</span>, the aspect of light, the aspect of life, etc. And all of this is through the <span class="key-term">ta'anis</span>, which is the aspect of <span class="key-term">tzedakah</span>, which is the aspect of the <span class="key-term">shekalim</span> of the donation for the <span class="key-term">Mishkan</span> — which were commanded after Yom HaKipurim — from which [derives] the essential miracle of Purim, to subdue Haman-Amalek, as mentioned above. And therefore, they begin with Parshas Shekalim.
</p>

<p>
And this is what is brought in the holy Zohar: that <strong>Purim is the aspect of Yom Kipurim</strong>. For the miracle of Purim — to subdue Amalek, who is the <span class="key-term">zuhama</span> and lust of the body — is through the <span class="key-term">ta'anis</span>, as mentioned above, which is the aspect of the fast of Yom HaKipurim. For the holiness of all the fasts receives from the fast of Yom HaKipurim. For the essential subduing of the four elements of the body, to elevate them from <span class="key-term">beheimah</span> to <span class="key-term">adam</span>, is through <span class="key-term">ta'anis</span>, as mentioned above.
</p>

<!-- SECTION 14 -->
<div class="section-heading">Section 14</div>

<p>
And therefore, <strong>all the holiness of the <span class="key-term">Mishkan</span> and the Bais HaMikdash was from the aspect of the <span class="key-term">ta'anis</span> of Yom HaKipurim</strong>. For only on the holy day of the fast — on the day of Yom HaKipurim — did the <span class="key-term">Kohen Gadol</span> [High Priest] enter <em>before and within</em> [<span class="key-term">lifnay v'lifnim</span>, i.e. the Holy of Holies], which is the highest holiness of all the ten holinesses. And from there all the holinesses are drawn. For there, between the staves [of the Ark], was the essential resting of the <span class="key-term">Shechinah</span>, which is the root of all holinesses, as it is stated: <span class="verse">"I am Hashem Who sanctifies you"</span> <span class="source">(Vayikra 20:8)</span>.
</p>

<p>
For the essential purpose of all the holinesses in the <span class="key-term">Mishkan</span> and the Bais HaMikdash was to subdue and nullify the <span class="key-term">zuhama</span> of the <span class="key-term">nachash</span>, which is the aspect of <span class="key-term">beheimah</span>, the aspect of body, etc., as mentioned above. For the essential purpose of the <span class="key-term">Mishkan</span> and the Bais HaMikdash was built to bring there all the <span class="key-term">korbanos</span> — which are of animals and birds — to subdue the spirit of animality, which is the aspect of body, etc. Through this, all the sins are atoned for — those that are drawn from there — whether the sins of each and every individual, or the sin of Adam HaRishon and the deed of the <span class="key-term">eigel</span>, which the <span class="key-term">Mishkan</span> and the Bais HaMikdash atone for, as is known <span class="source">(Tanchuma, Pekudei; brought in the commentary of Rashi)</span>. For all of them are drawn from the <span class="key-term">zuhama</span> of the <span class="key-term">nachash</span> that clings to the aspect of body, the aspect of <span class="key-term">beheimah</span>, the aspect of death, etc.
</p>

<p>
And therefore, all the power of the <span class="key-term">Mishkan</span> and the Bais HaMikdash to rectify and atone for all of this was drawn from the Holy of Holies — into which no person entered except the <span class="key-term">Kohen Gadol</span> on the day of the fast of Yom HaKipurim, which is the day of the <span class="key-term">ta'anis</span>. For this holiness — to subdue the body, the aspect of <span class="key-term">beheimah</span>, etc. — is impossible to draw down except through the <span class="key-term">ta'anis</span>, which is the aspect of breath of the mouth that has no sin — the breath of the schoolchildren, etc., as mentioned above.
</p>

<p>
For the schoolchildren receive the breath of their mouths from the <span class="key-term">Mishkan</span> and the Bais HaMikdash <span class="source">(as brought elsewhere in the Torah [discourse] "Azamrah l'Elokai b'Odi" in Siman 282)</span>. And they receive primarily from the Holy of Holies, from between the <span class="key-term">keruvim</span> — for the <span class="key-term">keruvim</span> are <span class="key-term">anpay zutray</span> [small faces] <span class="source">(Sukkah 5b)</span>, the aspect of schoolchildren. For from there they receive the breath of their holy mouths. And there the <span class="key-term">Kohen Gadol</span> enters on the day of the fast of Yom HaKipurim — to atone for Yisrael, to subdue the aspect of body, the aspect of <span class="key-term">beheimah</span>, etc. — and to elevate the soul, etc. — through the <span class="key-term">ta'anis</span> of Yom HaKipurim, which is the root of all fasts. For from there, from between the <span class="key-term">keruvim</span>, one draws the breath of the holy mouths of the schoolchildren that has no sin, through the <span class="key-term">ta'anis</span>. Through this, the body and the material, the aspect of <span class="key-term">beheimah</span>, etc., is subdued, and all the judgments are sweetened, and all the sins are atoned for, and the soul is elevated, etc. — which is the aspect of Torah, as mentioned above. Amen and Amen.
</p>

<!-- SECTION 15 -->
<div class="section-heading">Section 15</div>

<p>
And this is the aspect of the <strong>gifts to the <span class="key-term">Kohen</span></strong> [<span class="key-term">matnos kehunah</span>] — that one must give from the animal to the <span class="key-term">Kohen</span> <span class="source">(Yoreh Dai'ah, Siman 61)</span>. For the gifts that are given to the <span class="key-term">Kohen</span> from those who slaughter the sacrifice <span class="source">(see Devarim 18:3)</span> — this is the aspect of <span class="key-term">tzedakah</span> of Eretz Yisrael, which is the aspect of <span class="key-term">ta'anis</span>, through which is the essential subduing of the material and the body, the aspect of <span class="key-term">beheimah</span>, and the elevation of the soul, etc., the aspect of <span class="key-term">adam</span>, as mentioned above. For the <span class="key-term">Kohen</span> is in the aspect of the holiness of Eretz Yisrael, for the service of the <span class="key-term">Kohen</span> is in the Bais HaMikdash with the <span class="key-term">korbanos</span> — from which derives the essential holiness of Eretz Yisrael <span class="source">(as already brought many times)</span>.
</p>

<p>
And therefore, the gifts that Yisrael give to the <span class="key-term">Kohen</span> — to support him so that he can perform his service in the Bais HaMikdash, as explained in the Torah in many verses <span class="source">(and they are the twenty-four gifts of the <span class="key-term">kehunah</span>; Vayikra 6:9; Bamidbar 18:2–28)</span> — this is the aspect of <span class="key-term">tzedakah</span> of Eretz Yisrael, whose holiness is drawn from the Bais HaMikdash, which is the aspect of the holiness of the <span class="key-term">Kohen</span>, as mentioned above. And therefore, one must give from the animal the gifts to the <span class="key-term">Kohen</span>, which is the aspect of <span class="key-term">tzedakah</span> of Eretz Yisrael. For <span class="key-term">tzedakah</span> of Eretz Yisrael is the aspect of <span class="key-term">ta'anis</span>, through which is the essential elevation of the soul, etc. — from <span class="key-term">beheimah</span> to <span class="key-term">adam</span> <span class="source">(as mentioned above in the above-mentioned Torah [discourse])</span>.
</p>

<p>
And therefore, one gives to the <span class="key-term">Kohen</span> <strong>the foreleg, the jaw, and the stomach</strong> [<span class="key-term">zeroah, lechayayim, v'kayvah</span>]. And it is found in the words of our Sages of blessed memory that <strong>the foreleg</strong> corresponds to <span class="verse">"and he took a spear,"</span> etc. <span class="source">(Bamidbar 25:7)</span>. It follows that all these gifts to the <span class="key-term">Kohen</span> are in the merit of Pinchas, who was zealous regarding the deed of Zimri. For Pinchas was zealous with the zealotry of Hashem Tzevakos against Bilam the wicked — for the deed of Zimri with the daughters of Moav was by the counsel of Bilam, as it is written: <span class="verse">"behold, they were the ones who, by the counsel of Bilam,"</span> etc. <span class="source">(Bamidbar 31:16)</span>. And Bilam is the aspect of Amalek <span class="source">(see Sefer HaGilgulim, Chapter 49)</span>, for they are intertwined in their letters <span class="source">(as brought in the holy books)</span>. For Bilam and Amalek are essentially the <span class="key-term">zuhama</span> of the <span class="key-term">nachash</span>, which is the lust of the body and the material, the lust of the <span class="key-term">yetzer hara</span> — whose essential strengthening is in the lust of licentiousness [<span class="key-term">ni'uf</span>], as it is written in the holy Zohar and brought in the books of Rabbeinu of blessed memory: <em>"the essence of the evil inclination is to uncover nakedness, and that is the essence of the impurity"</em> <span class="source">(see Likutay Moharan, end of Siman 2)</span>.
</p>

<p>
And the essential battle between the soul and the body is in this lust. For the <span class="key-term">bris kodesh</span> [holy covenant] is the channel through which all souls emerge, for the essential procreation is through it. And one who merits to sanctify himself in his marital union properly — then he produces holy offspring, in a manner that the soul overpowers the body. And vice versa, G-d forbid. And therefore, there is the essential battle, as is known.
</p>

<p>
And therefore, Pinchas — who was zealous regarding the deed of Zimri and subdued the lust of licentiousness, which is the aspect of the <span class="key-term">zuhama</span> of Bilam-Amalek, and was zealous with the zealotry of the <span class="key-term">bris</span>, and subdued the material and the body, the aspect of <span class="key-term">beheimah</span>, and strengthened and elevated the soul, the aspect of <span class="key-term">adam</span> — for its essential elevation is through the sanctity of the <span class="key-term">bris</span>, as mentioned above — therefore, Pinchas merited that his brothers and sons, the <span class="key-term">kohanim</span>, would receive the foreleg, the jaw, and the stomach from those who slaughter the sacrifice — corresponding to <span class="verse">"and he took a spear,"</span> etc., and corresponding to <span class="verse">"and Pinchas stood and prayed,"</span> etc. <span class="source">(Tehillim 106:30; see Chullin 134b)</span>. For all the zealotry of Pinchas, to subdue the <span class="key-term">zuhama</span> of Bilam-Amalek, is the aspect of subduing the material, the aspect of <span class="key-term">beheimah</span>, etc.
</p>

<p>
And therefore, those who slaughter the sacrifice — who need to subdue the aspect of animality, the aspect of the material, etc. — and to elevate the soul from <span class="key-term">chai</span> to <span class="key-term">medaber</span>: therefore they must give the gifts to the <span class="key-term">Kohen</span> — the foreleg, the jaw, and the stomach — corresponding to the deed of Pinchas, in order to draw down the holiness of Pinchas, who merited to subdue the <span class="key-term">zuhama</span> of the material — which is the <span class="key-term">zuhama</span> of Bilam, etc. — and to elevate the soul, etc., as mentioned above. For this is the essential rectification of the animal, as mentioned above — which those who slaughter the sacrifice, who are the slaughterers, must accomplish, as mentioned above. And therefore they must give the gifts — foreleg and jaw, etc. — corresponding to the deed of Pinchas specifically, as mentioned above.
</p>

<!-- SECTION 16 -->
<div class="section-heading">Section 16</div>

<p>
Also, the foreleg, the jaw, and the stomach — which are the aspect of <span class="key-term">tzedakah</span> of Eretz Yisrael — are in the aspect of <span class="key-term">ta'anis</span>, through which the soul ascends from <span class="key-term">chai</span> to <span class="key-term">medaber</span>, as mentioned above. For this soul that is in the living creature is a reincarnated soul of an actual person, who had the vessels of speech, and through his sins it descended from the level of <span class="key-term">medaber</span> to the level of <span class="key-term">chai</span>. And therefore, one must give the <strong>foreleg and the jaw</strong> to the <span class="key-term">Kohen</span> — for they are the aspect of the <em>vessels of speech</em> in man. For in the <strong>jaw</strong> of a person, all five <em>articulation points of the mouth</em> are fixed. And this is what our Sages of blessed memory were precise about and said: that one must give <strong>the jaw with the tongue that is between them</strong> — with the tongue specifically. For the essential intention is to rectify the tongue and the speech — to elevate the soul that fell to animality, to the absence of speech — to elevate it to the level of <span class="key-term">adam</span>, which is the aspect of <span class="key-term">medaber</span>, as mentioned above.
</p>

<p>
And the <strong>foreleg</strong> — this is the aspect of the root of speech. For the root of speech is drawn from the five fingers of the left arm. And even though regarding an animal one must give the <em>right</em> foreleg specifically, but it is known that the totality of the lower level in relation to the upper level is in the aspect of the <em>left</em> of the upper level. All the more so regarding an animal in relation to man — for the entire level of <span class="key-term">beheimah</span>, even its right side, is still far from the left side of <span class="key-term">adam</span>. And if only we could elevate from the right foreleg of the animal to the left arm of man, which is the root of speech, as mentioned above.
</p>

<p>
And the <strong>stomach</strong> [<span class="key-term">kayvah</span>] — this is the essential vessel that cooks and receives the food that is eaten. And therefore, by giving the stomach of the animal to the <span class="key-term">Kohen</span> — which is the aspect of <span class="key-term">tzedakah</span> of Eretz Yisrael — through this one draws the holiness of the <span class="key-term">ta'anis</span>. Which is this very aspect: that one subdues the <em>food of the animal</em> — which is the craving for eating — and elevates the eating to holiness through <span class="key-term">tzedakah</span> of Eretz Yisrael, which is the aspect of the elevation of the soul, as mentioned above.
</p>

<p>
For the essential [purpose of the] <span class="key-term">ta'anis</span> is to subdue and break the craving for eating, through which the person lives — for the vitality of all souls is through eating, as we say in the final blessing: <span class="verse">"for all that You created, to sustain with them the soul of every living being,"</span> etc. And therefore, there is the essential place of clarification — from <span class="key-term">beheimah</span> to <span class="key-term">adam</span>. And therefore, when one wants to subdue the body and elevate the soul from <span class="key-term">beheimah</span> to <span class="key-term">adam</span>, one needs <span class="key-term">ta'anis</span> — to break the craving for eating, which is the vitality of the soul — to break one's material cravings, so that through this one merits to elevate the soul, which is the vitality, from <span class="key-term">beheimah</span> to <span class="key-term">adam</span>.
</p>

<p>
And therefore, the essential [purpose of the] <span class="key-term">ta'anis</span> is the <span class="key-term">tzedakah</span>, the aspect of <span class="key-term">"agra d'sa'anisa tzidkasa"</span> <span class="source">(as mentioned above in the above-mentioned Torah [discourse])</span>. For in truth, a <em>human meal</em> [<span class="key-term">ma'achal adam</span>] is very precious — that is, when one eats as a man and not as an animal — meaning, when one eats with the holiness of a Jewish person who is called <span class="key-term">adam</span> — then certainly his eating is very precious, for specifically through his eating his soul is elevated, in the aspect of: <span class="verse">"the righteous eats to satisfy his soul"</span> <span class="source">(Mishlei 13:25)</span>. And then he merits through his eating specifically to elevate the soul, the aspect of <span class="key-term">adam</span>, and to subdue the body, the aspect of <span class="key-term">beheimah</span>, etc.
</p>

<p>
But one who has not yet merited this, and his eating is not in the aspect of a <em>human meal</em> — then the body and material remain in their full force, G-d forbid — and then he needs <span class="key-term">ta'anis</span>. And the essential [purpose of the] <span class="key-term">ta'anis</span> is the <span class="key-term">tzedakah</span> — that is, he must fast, to break the craving for eating, the craving of the body. And through the <span class="key-term">tzedakah</span> that he gives to a worthy poor person — especially <span class="key-term">tzedakah</span> of Eretz Yisrael, sustaining the souls of the poor of Eretz Yisrael — through this, the soul is elevated. For he gives the portion of his eating — what he would have needed to eat on the day of the fast — he gives it to the poor person to sustain the soul of the poor person, as is brought in the holy books, that this is how one should conduct oneself on the day of a fast.
</p>

<p>
It follows that the essential [purpose of the] <span class="key-term">ta'anis</span> is to subdue the craving for eating and to take the portion of his eating to give to a worthy poor person. Through this, one subdues the material and elevates the soul from <span class="key-term">chai</span> to <span class="key-term">medaber</span>, from <span class="key-term">beheimah</span> to <span class="key-term">adam</span>, as mentioned above. And this is the aspect of giving the <strong>stomach</strong> to the <span class="key-term">Kohen</span>, which is the essential vessel of eating. And through giving the stomach — the eating vessel of the animal — to the <span class="key-term">Kohen</span>, which is the aspect of <span class="key-term">tzedakah</span> of Eretz Yisrael, through this one draws the holiness of the <span class="key-term">ta'anis</span>. Which is this very aspect: that one subdues the eating of the animal — which is the craving for eating — and elevates the eating to holiness through <span class="key-term">tzedakah</span> of Eretz Yisrael, which is the aspect of the elevation of the soul, as mentioned above.
</p>

<p><em>[Note: This pertains to what was said above.]</em></p>

<!-- SECTION 17 -->
<div class="section-heading">Section 17</div>

<p>
And therefore, Haman made <strong>a gallows fifty <span class="key-term">amos</span> high</strong> <span class="source">(Esther 5:14; 7:10)</span>. For he intended to overpower against the holiness of Yom HaKipurim, which is the day of the <span class="key-term">ta'anis</span> — which is in the secret of the great <span class="key-term">Yovel</span> [Jubilee], which is the fiftieth year, whose essential holiness is on Yom HaKipurim — and it is the secret of the World to Come, when the great <span class="key-term">shofar</span> will be sounded. For all of this is the aspect of <span class="key-term">teshuvah</span>, the aspect of <span class="key-term">ta'anis</span>, through which one subdues the material, etc. And Haman wanted to overpower against all of this.
</p>

<p>
And therefore he cast a <span class="key-term">pur</span> — that is, the lot <span class="source">(Esther 3:7; 9:24)</span> — to overpower, G-d forbid, against the lot of Yom HaKipurim, as it is stated: <span class="verse">"and Aharon shall place upon the two he-goats lots — one lot for Hashem,"</span> etc. <span class="source">(Vayikra 16:8)</span>. But Hashem, blessed is He, in His mercy, gave Yisrael the power to overpower him through the holiness of the fasts that they fasted, as it is stated: <span class="verse">"and the matters of the fasts and their outcry,"</span> etc. — as mentioned above. For through the <span class="key-term">ta'anis</span> one subdues the material, etc., which is the aspect of the <span class="key-term">zuhama</span> of Haman-Amalek, and elevates the soul, etc.
</p>

<p>
And therefore, he was hanged specifically on this gallows that was fifty <span class="key-term">amos</span> high <span class="source">(Esther 5:14; 7:10)</span>. For his downfall was from there specifically — through the fact that they merited to fast until they drew through the fasts the holiness of Yom HaKipurim, the aspect of <span class="key-term">Yovel</span>, the aspect of the fiftieth year <span class="source">(Vayikra 25:11)</span>. And through this, he and his seed fell, and they hanged him and his sons on this very gallows. And the <span class="key-term">pur</span> of Haman was reversed to become our <span class="key-term">pur</span>. And therefore, these days were called <span class="verse">"Purim"</span> specifically — on account of the <span class="key-term">pur</span> <span class="source">(Esther 9:26)</span>. For the lot was reversed upon him. For through the fasts, they drew the holiness of Yom HaKipurim, the holiness of the lot of Yom HaKipurim. For his essential subduing was through the <span class="key-term">ta'anis</span>, etc., as mentioned above.
</p>

<p>
And this is what is brought in the holy Zohar <span class="source">(Tikkunay Zohar, Tikkun 21, page 57b)</span>: that <strong>Purim is in the aspect of Yom HaKipurim</strong>, as mentioned above.
</p>

<!-- TRANSLATOR'S SUMMARY -->
<div class="translator-summary">
  <h3>Translator's Summary</h3>

  <p>
    <em>Shechitah 4</em> is the longest section — seventeen subsections — built upon <strong>Likutay Moharan I:37</strong> (<em>"Dirshu Hashem v'Uzo"</em>). Its central axis is the cosmic battle between <em>chomer</em> (material/body = <em>beheimah</em> = darkness = death = forgetfulness = external wisdoms) and <em>tzurah</em> (form/soul = <em>adam</em> = light = life = memory = Torah). The weapon that tips this battle is the <em>ta'anis</em> (fast), whose essence is <em>tzedakah</em> — especially of Eretz Yisrael — which is the breath of the schoolchildren without sin. <em>Shechitah</em> is not merely a dietary act but a metaphysical one: every animal harbors a reincarnated human soul that fell there through sin, and the act of ritual slaughter, performed with proper intention, elevates that soul from <em>chai</em> (mere animal life) back to <em>medaber</em> (the human/speaking level).
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>(1)</strong> Five laws of <em>shechitah</em> = five aspects of the soul = five bloods = five letters of <em>Mantzepach</em>. <strong>(2)</strong> Knife shown to the <em>chacham</em> = Torah wisdom = <em>kavod</em> of holiness = root of soul-elevation; non-Jew/<em>mumar</em> cannot slaughter validly. <strong>(3)</strong> <em>Oso v'es beno</em>: the <em>beirur</em> happens day by day, generation by generation — 600,000 souls die and are born daily = a new generation each day (<em>Koheles Rabbah</em>); "that I command you <em>today</em>" + "teach your children" = same idea; kavod of Hashem revealed anew daily. <strong>(4)</strong> Therefore two generations cannot be clarified on one day. <strong>(5)</strong> A fast = minimum one full day, corresponding to the <em>middos</em>/days structure. <strong>(6)</strong> <em>Shekalim</em> = <em>tzedakah</em> after Yom Kipurim = <em>Mishkan</em> = holiness of Eretz Yisrael = <em>hevel peh</em> of schoolchildren = <em>keruvim</em>. <strong>(7)</strong> Amalek defeated through <em>shekalim</em>/<em>ta'anis</em> in every generation. <strong>(8)</strong> Haman's lot reversed — the day of greatest danger became the day of salvation; <strong><em>understand this for practical application — your eternal life depends on it — there is no despair in the world, ever.</em></strong> <strong>(9)</strong> <em>Treifah</em>: only man (possessor of <em>b'chirah</em>) can subdue the Side of Death; knife = <em>ta'anis</em> = <em>dibbur</em> = <em>cherev la'Hashem</em>; Eisav/Amalek appointed over the sword; the fast is harder than the sword. <strong>(10)</strong> Voice of schoolchildren defeats Eisav; through the fast, the sword is taken from Amalek's own hand. <strong>(11)</strong> "Slaughter with this" (<em>Shmuel I</em> 14) — derived after a fast. <strong>(12)</strong> <em>Nevailah/treifah</em> forbidden; <em>tzadikim</em> alive even in death; only a being with free choice can nullify the Side of Death. <strong>(13)</strong> Four <em>parshiyos</em> of Adar = subduing four kingdoms / four elements; Purim = Yom Kipurim (Zohar). <strong>(14)</strong> <em>Kohen Gadol</em> enters Holy of Holies only on the fast of Yom Kipurim; <em>keruvim</em> = <em>anpay zutray</em> = schoolchildren; all holiness flows from <em>ta'anis</em>. <strong>(15)</strong> <em>Matnos kehunah</em> (foreleg, jaw, stomach) = merit of Pinchas subduing Bilam-Amalek = <em>zuhama</em> of the serpent = lust of licentiousness; <em>bris</em> = channel of souls = site of the essential battle. <strong>(16)</strong> Jaw + tongue = vessels of speech; foreleg = root of speech (five fingers of left arm); stomach = fast breaks the craving for eating; a <em>tzadik</em>'s eating itself elevates the soul. <strong>(17)</strong> Haman's gallows 50 <em>amos</em> = opposing the 50th year/<em>Yovel</em>/Yom Kipurim; reversed upon him; Purim = Yom Kipurim.
  </p>

